    "lawCode": "Amd §2801-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3912\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to school safety plans\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo have school districts formulate policies and procedures for\nresponding to a student's implied or direct intention to commit\nsuicide and to have policies and procedures for contacting parents,\nguardians or persons in parental relation to a student if the student\nmakes an implied or direct intention to commit suicide\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Section 1 adds a new paragraph b-1 to subdivision 2 of\nsection 2801-a of the education law by requiring school districts to\ninclude policies and procedures for responding to a student's implied\nor direct intention to commit suicide.\n\nSection 2: Section 2 adds a new paragraph f to subdivision 2 of\nsection 2801-a of the education law by requiring school districts to\ninclude policies and procedures for contacting parents, guardians or\npersons in parental relation to a student of the district that\nindicates an implied or direct intention to commit suicide.\n\nSection 2: Section 3 sets forth an immediate effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSchool districts and BOCES are required to establish school safety\nplans (\"SSPs\") in accordance with section 2801-a of the education law.\nThe SSPs are designed to have established protocols and procedures in\ninstances of crisis intervention and emergency response and\nmanagement. In addition, the SSPs require in such instances to have\ndefined policies and protocols to notify parents and/or guardians of\nstudents in such crisis and emergency situations. While SSPs are\nexpansive in their coverage of instances such as threats of violence,\nactual acts of violence, bomb threats, hostage takings, and the like,\nthere is a glaring deficiency in regards to a student's threat to\ncommit suicide.\n\nSome school districts and BOCES have policies and protocols in place\nin the event a student makes an implied or direct intention to commit\nsuicide, but there is no statutory requirement for a school district\nor BOCES to do so. Perhaps even more alarming is there is no\nrequirement that if a student makes an implied or direct intention to\ncommit suicide there is no requirement that the school district or\nBOCES contact the student's parent or guardian.\n\nIn one instance in particular, a student indicated her desire to\ncommit suicide to a teacher on the day before the schools winter\nbreak. The district never contacted the student's parent and the\nparent was not made aware of her daughters intention until many days\nlater. Thankfully the student did not follow through on her threat to\n\n\ncommit suicide, But the fact of the matter is the parent was never\nmade aware by the school district and that is simply unacceptable.\n\nThis legislation would require school districts and BOCES to include,\nin their already required SSPs, policies and procedures be put in\nplace in the event a student makes an implied or direct intention to\ncommit suicide. Moreover, it would require there to be policies and\nprocedures in place to ensure a parent or guardian is made aware of\nthe student's implied or direct intention to commit suicide.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2015: New bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
