    "lawCode": "Amd §2801-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3912A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nschool safety plans\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation will ensure that school districts formulate policies\nand procedures for responding to a student where it is reasonable to\nbelieve is at risk of harming himself or herself.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill adds a new paragraph b-1 to education law\nsection 2801-a.2 to require school districts to include policies and\nprocedures for responding to a student where it is reasonable to\nbelieve that the student is at risk of harming himself or herself as\nmanifested by a threat of suicide or other conduct demonstrating that\nthe student is a danger to himself or herself.\n\nSection 2 reletters paragraphs and adds a new paragraph f to education\nlaw section 2801-a.2 to require school districts to include policies\nand procedures for contacting parents, guardians or persons in\nparental relation to a student of the district where it is reasonable\nto believe that the student is at risk of harming himself or herself\nas manifested by a threat of suicide or other conduct demonstrating\nthat the student is a danger to himself or herself.\n\nSection 3 is the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSchool districts and BOCES are required to establish school safety\nplans (\"SSPs\") in accordance with education law section 2801-a. The\nSSPs establish protocols and procedures in instances of crisis\nintervention and emergency response and management. In addition, the\nSSPs are required to have defined policies and protocols to notify\nparents and/or guardians of students in such crisis and emergency\nsituations. While SSPs address many matters of student safety\nincluding threats or acts of violence, bomb threats, hostage takings,\nand the like, these plans do not address a student's threat to commit\nsuicide.\n\nWhile, some school districts and BOCES have policies and protocols in\nplace in the event a student threatens suicide or exhibits other\nconduct demonstrating the student is a danger to himself or herself,\nthere is no statutory requirement for a school district or BOCES to do\nso. Equally as alarming is there is no requirement for a school\ndistrict or BOCES to notify a student's parent or guardian if a\nstudent threatens suicide or exhibits such behavior.\n\nIn one instance, a student indicated her desire to commit suicide to a\nteacher on the day before the school's winter break. The district\nnever contacted the student's parent and the parent was not made aware\nof her daughter's intention until many days later. Fortunately, the\nstudent did not follow through on her threat. However, the fact that\n\n\nthe parent was not made aware by the school of the daughter's\nintention at the time it occurred is simply unacceptable.\n\nThis legislation would require school districts and BOCES to include,\nin their already required SSPs, policies and procedures for responding\nto a student where it is reasonable to believe that the student is at\nrisk of harm due to a threat of suicide or other behavior that\ndemonstrates the student is a threat to himself or herself. Moreover,\nit would also require policies and procedures to ensure a parent or\nguardian is made aware of the student's threat of suicide or behavior\ndemonstrating that the student is a risk to himself or herself in a\ntimely manner.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
